Package: openmux-factory-tools
Version: 1.3.0
Architecture: all
Maintainer: OpenMux <support@openmux.io>
Homepage: https://github.com/OpenMux/openmux-img
Section: misc
Priority: optional
Depends: ftdi-eeprom, python3, python3-serial, sudo, usbutils
Description: OpenMux factory provisioning and test tools
 Standalone factory utilities for the OpenMux console: programming the
 internal/external FTDI EEPROM (manufacturer, product, serial base),
 a topology scan that tells the console's own FTDI chips apart from a
 plug-in EX-12 adapter by where each chip hangs on USB (they share one
 VID:PID, and an unprogrammed chip has no serial to tell them apart
 by), in a full report and a compact --summary report (every chip in
 one table, the console's own included, plus one line per card) which
 is what the install medium's menu prints as its header, an audit that
 checks every /dev/openmux/portN against the
 USB interface the udev rules promise it, a serial loopback test and
 the board test runner. The flashers refuse the console's chips when
 handed a card and refuse a card when handed the console, and re-read
 each chip immediately before writing it because flashing re-numbers
 the hub. Installed on the install medium (used from the factory
 menu) and on the target (kept for field service). Dependencies are the
 programs these scripts exec: ftdi_eeprom (distro package ftdi-eeprom,
 hyphenated name, underscored binary), python3, python3-serial for
 serial-test.py, and sudo - the flashers name it as argv[0] unconditionally, so
 it is needed even when they already run as root. Declared on the package
 rather than as image config lines so any unit that can run these tools can run
 the flashers too. bash and coreutils are deliberately NOT declared: both are
 Essential: yes in Debian and present by policy. usbutils is the one declared
 dependency nothing here execs - the scanner reads sysfs instead of lsusb - and
 it is kept because it is the next thing a technician reaches for.
